FINDING 1 (non-blocking, reported for the lead): the in-code guard
`math.Abs(base+adjustment-livingExpenseAmount) < 0.005` in budget_fit.go is inert.
`adjustment` is defined as `livingExpenseAmount - base` one line earlier, so the
condition is algebraically always true and can never suppress anything.
Demonstrated: mutation C replaced it with `if true {` and
`go test -count=1 ./internal/services/retirement/analysis/ ./internal/templates/`
still exits 0 (both "ok"). The brief's "suppressed when the identity doesn't hold
(decline-rate cases)" is nonetheless satisfied, by a different mechanism: read
internal/services/retirement/engine/expense.go L17-26 — when SpendingPhaseConfig
is enabled the decline rate is not applied at all (phase branch returns
`MonthlyLivingExpenses * multiplier * inflationFactor`), and
`compoundedFactorFromPercent(rate, 0) == 1.0` (math.go L30-35), so at month 0 the
identity holds exactly whenever sub-items are emitted. The decline-rate path is
covered by TestBudgetFit_LivingExpensesSubItems_AbsentWhenPhasesDisabled, which
sets SpendingDeclineRate=1.0 and asserts absence. The guard is dead code, not a
correctness gap.

FINDING 2 (non-blocking): the sum-invariant assertion is tautological given the
implementation (mutA changed base and the sum still balanced). Non-vacuity of the
tests rests on the absolute-figure assertions, which did kill mutA.

